<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yangzhou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yangzhou is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> meets the river. As Jiangdu it was the favourite southern residence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-yang-of-sui">Emperor Yang of Sui</a>, who was murdered there in 618. Under the Tang and later dynasties it was a wealthy centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a> and commerce, and it was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> in 1645.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yangzhou.jpg" alt="Yangzhou" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gisling · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Five_Pavilion_bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yangzhou is a prefecture-level city in central Jiangsu Province, on the north bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. It borders the provincial capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> to the south-west, Huai'an to the north, Yancheng to the north-east and Taizhou to the east, and Zhenjiang lies across the river. It stands on a plain with many streams and lakes, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> crosses the prefecture from north to south. The name means Rising Prefecture and refers to its former position as the capital of the ancient Yangzhou prefecture. The climate is subtropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, with winters of about four months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Spring and Autumn period Fuchai, the hegemon of Wu, built the Han Canal to improve supply lines from the region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>, where he was fighting Qi. By 486 BCE the canal joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> at present-day Yangzhou to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a> at present-day Huai'an. The next year Fuchai founded a fortress, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hancheng">Hancheng</a>, to guard its southern end. It was a square of 3 li by 3 li raised about 12 metres above the water on the Yangtze's northern bank, with the canal forming a moat on two sides.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a> the area formed the Guangling Commandery of Xu Province, whose seat, also called Guangling, lay near the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and the Han Canal. Historically Yangzhou was one of the wealthiest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, known at various periods for its great merchant families, poets, artists and scholars. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a> in 1853 to the 1980s it declined through war damage, the neglect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> as railways replaced it, and stagnation. It endured eight years of Japanese occupation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a>, and in 1943 about 1,200 Allied civilians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> were interned there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Yangzhou had a population of 4,559,797 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The Runyang Yangtze River Bridge carries the road to Zhenjiang, and the harbour at the junction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a> and the Yangtze handles passengers and freight for northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, eastern Anhui and south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>. Yangzhou Railway Station opened in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yangzhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The canal linked Luoyang with Chang'an, with the agricultural centres towards Jiangdu (now <b>Yangzhou</b>) and Yuhang (now Hangzhou), and with the northern frontiers near modern Beijing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Work began in 486 BCE south of <b>Yangzhou</b>, and within three years it had connected the Yangtze with the Huai River, using existing waterways, lakes and marshes.</blockquote><a href="/work/grand-canal-of-china">Grand Canal of China · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Rich merchants with official connections built huge fortunes and patronised literature, theatre and the arts; the salt merchants of <b>Yangzhou</b> dealt directly with the Imperial Household Department.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li><li><blockquote>In 760, in revenge, Chinese rebels killed thousands of Arabs and Persians in the <b>Yangzhou</b> massacre.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-30">30 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Nanjing fell in 1645 after the massacre at <b>Yangzhou</b>, the Longwu Emperor, enthroned at Fuzhou, was captured in Fujian in 1646, and the Yongli Emperor ruled from Zhaoqing and later the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a></li><li><blockquote>Yang Xingmi, a soldier of humble origins, won control of the Huainan region between the Huai and Yangtze rivers in the 890s and made <b>Yangzhou</b> his base.</blockquote><a href="/state/wu-ten-kingdoms">Wu (Ten Kingdoms)</a></li></ul></section>
